---

Native `h1`–`h6` carry their role by default. Any other element takes a role as an identity token: `data-ui="text-h4"`, `data-ui="badge text-xs"`. A role stacks with other tokens, and a utility overrides one property of it (`--font-size`, `--leading`, `--font-weight`); never rebuild a role from utilities.

{% roles /%}

## Sizes

The body sizes `text-2xs` to `text-2xl` are the `--font-size-*` tokens with matching leading and tracking. Every size comes from a fluid type scale: a base size and a scale ratio, each growing between a small-screen and a large-screen value, with per-step multipliers below the base. Tune those controls (on the [tokens](/api/foundations/tokens/) page), not the sizes. `text-eyebrow` is a small label: uppercase, tracked, and muted by convention. `text-display` is the one size above `h1`, for a hero headline.

## Letter spacing and line height

A typeface with wide or tight default spacing needs every role adjusted by the same amount. Do it with one token instead of thirteen. `--tracking-modifier` is a length added to every `--tracking-*` token. `--tracking-heading-modifier` covers `display` and `h1`–`h6`, and `--tracking-body-modifier` covers `2xl`–`2xs` and the eyebrow. Both default to `--tracking-modifier`, so set the global one and override a group only when it needs something different. Give each a unit (`0.01em`). A bare `0` would break the `calc()` behind the tokens.

Line height works the same way with multipliers. `--leading-heading-multiplier` and `--leading-body-multiplier` (both `1` by default) scale every `--leading-*` token in their group.

```css
:root {
  --tracking-modifier: -0.01em;
  --leading-body-multiplier: 1.05;
}
```

## Links

Links inside `prose` are underlined for you. Anywhere else, an inline link takes the `text-link` role: `<a href="/docs/" data-ui="text-link">`. It underlines in the text's own color at the `--decoration-thickness` and `--decoration-offset` tokens and fades the underline on hover. It stacks with a size role (`data-ui="text-sm text-link"`). A link that acts like a control is a button with the `link` variant.

## Rich text

`data-ui="prose"` styles the content inside it: paragraph rhythm, lists, figures, blockquotes, headings. Give it a reading measure with `--max-w: var(--article-lg); --mx: auto`. `data-prose-size="sm"` and `"lg"` resize one article, and `--ui-prose-flow` loosens or tightens its spacing; both are on the [prose](/api/primitives/prose/) page with its hooks.
